.TH ACV 1 "August 2026" "acv" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
acv \- manipulate Photoshop ACV curve and AMP pencil curve files
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B acv
.I command
.RI [ options ]
.I args ...
.PP
Common patterns:
.PP
.B acv show
.RB [ \-w " width" ]
.I curve
.RI [ reference ]
.PP
.B acv
.I transform
.RB [ \-s ]
.RB [ \-p ]
.RB [ \-q ]
.RB [ \-v " pivot" ]
.I ...
.RI [ output ]
.PP
.B acv
.PP
With no arguments at all,
.B acv
starts an interactive prompt (see
.B INTERACTIVE MODE
below).
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B acv
is a command-line utility (single Python script) for reading, inspecting, and transforming
Photoshop tonal curves. It supports Photoshop Curves preset files
.RB ( .acv ),
Photoshop pencil-curve files
.RB ( .amp ),
and a CSV interchange format
.RB ( .csv )
for real-valued workflows.
.PP
Input format is inferred from filename extension:
.RB .amp ,
.RB .acv ,
otherwise CSV.
.PP
Special filenames:
.TP
.B \-
Read CSV from stdin or write CSV to stdout.
.TP
.B %
As an output filename, display the curve instead of writing a file.
.PP
If an output argument is omitted for non-display commands, acv chooses a default:
.B %
when stdout is a TTY (interactive), otherwise
.B \-
for pipeline-friendly stdout output.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-s ", " \-\-select
Reduce the result curve to (up to) 16 control points using the script's greedy
selection method (Photoshop's ACV limit). Note: writing
.B .acv
also samples to 16 points regardless of this flag.
.TP
.BR \-p ", " \-\-preserve
Preserve x-values from the first input curve when producing an output curve.
This is useful when the first input has a specific x-grid (e.g., measured or sparse points)
and you want the output to keep those x locations rather than a filled 0..255 grid.
.TP
.BR \-q ", " \-\-quiet
Suppress debug output (toggles the script's DEBUG printing).
.TP
.BR \-w ", " \-\-width " " \fIwidth\fR
Set the terminal plot width (used by
.BR show ).
Height is derived as approximately one third of width.
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-pivot " " VALUE
Set the pivot used by
.BR contrast .
Default: 128.
.SH COMMANDS
.TP
.B show
.RI " <curve> " [ <reference> ]
Display a terminal plot of channel 0. If a reference is provided, its filled curve
is used as the background.
.TP
.B identity
.RI " <output>"
Write an identity curve.
.TP
.B offset
.RI " <offset> <input> " [ <output> ]
Add a constant offset to output values.
.TP
.B min
.RI " <new x=0 value> <input> " [ <output> ]
Shift so the curve's x=0 output becomes the given value.
.TP
.B max
.RI " <new x=255 value> <input> " [ <output> ]
Change contrast by scaling outputs so that input 255 maps to the given value,
while leaving input 0 unchanged.
.TP
.B contrast
.RI " <factor> <input> " [ <output> ]
Change contrast about a pivot:
.IR y' " = pivot + factor*(y-pivot)."
The pivot is 128 unless changed with
.BR \-v .
.TP
.B gamma
.RI " <gamma> <input> " [ <output> ]
Gamma applied to the filled mapping:
.IR y' " = 255*(y/255)^gamma."
.TP
.B linear
.RI " <error> <input> " [ <output_model> ]
Fit a straight line to curve points that are at least an
.I error
fraction away from 0 and 255 (near the ends), and output the fitted model curve.
.TP
.B compose
.RI " <curve1> <curve2> " [ <output> ]
Compose two curves (apply one after the other). Returns
.IR curve2 " \[u2218] " curve1
(i.e.,
.IR curve2 " \[u2218] " curve1 ,
curve2 after curve1).
.TP
.B diff
.RI " <curve1> <curve2> " [ <output> ]
Calculate
.IR 128 " + " curve1 " - " curve2 .
.TP
.B error
.RI " <curve1> <curve2>"
Print the maximum absolute difference between two curves over channels and x=0..255.
(Output is a number, not a curve.)
.TP
.B at
.RI " <x> <curve>"
Print the output value for a given input x (channel 0).
.TP
.B divide
.RI " <original> <divider> " [ <output residual> ]
Divide original by divider by composing with inverse(divider) so that:
.IR compose(divider, residual) " == " original .
Equivalently:
.IR residual " = divider^{-1} \[u2218] original ."
.TP
.B decompose
.RI " <gamma> <input> " [ <output_model> ]
Model input as a gamma curve (usually 2.2) composed with a linear curve:
.IR model " = g \[u2218] linear ."
(Typically used to approximate an input curve with two simpler components.)
.TP
.B adjust
.RI " <curve1> <curve2> " [ <output> ]
Combine two curves by adding curve2's deviation from identity to curve1.
.TP
.B inverse
.RI " <input> " [ <output> ]
Invert a curve by flipping it about the line y=x.
.TP
.B invert
.RI " <input> " [ <output> ]
Flip a curve about the line x=128, i.e. reverse its input axis. This simulates
the effect of inverting an image before printing it.
Not to be confused with
.BR inverse .
.TP
.B smooth
.RI " <points> <input> " [ <output> ]
Smooth a curve by fitting a spline with the given number of points.
.TP
.B average
.RI " <span> <input> " [ <output> ]
Smooth a curve by averaging points within
.I span
of x.
.TP
.B gradient
.RI " <span> <input> " [ <output> ]
Write 128 plus the local gradient of the curve, estimated at each x by averaging
the slope between every pair of points within
.I span
of x. A curve with slope 1 comes out flat at 129; steeper sections read higher
and flatter sections lower. Useful for seeing where a measured response loses
separation.
.SH FILE FORMATS
.TP
.B .acv
Binary control-point format. Output values are clamped to 0..255 when writing.
When writing, the curve is sampled down to 16 points (Photoshop-friendly).
.TP
.B .amp
Binary 256-byte mapping per curve (one byte per input level). Multiple curves may be concatenated.
.TP
.B .csv
Text format with header:
.BR channel,x,y .
The header is optional on input and is recognised rather than assumed, so a file
of bare
.I channel,x,y
rows reads correctly. Lines beginning with
.B #
are treated as comments and ignored.
For predictable reading, rows should be grouped by channel index.
CSV output is not clipped, in x or in y. A curve may be defined outside 0..255:
the inverse of a curve whose output leaves that range is, and so is the residual
from
.BR divide .
Writing such a curve to
.B .acv
or
.B .amp
keeps only the 0..255 part, because those formats cannot hold the rest.
.SH INTERACTIVE MODE
Run
.B acv
with no arguments to print the usage text and drop into a prompt. Each line is a
command exactly as it would be typed on the shell command line.
.PP
Results are kept rather than written to files. Each command that would have
written a curve stores it as
.BR $1 ", " $2 ,
and so on, and those names can be used as inputs to later commands.
.B %
stands for the most recent result, so transformations chain without naming
intermediate files:
.PP
.nf
acv $1> gamma 2.2 measured.csv
acv $2> average 4 %
acv $3> inverse %
acv $4> show % measured.csv
.fi
.PP
Command names tab-complete.
.B h
or
.B history
lists the stored results with the command that produced each,
.B help
reprints the usage text, and
.BR q ", " quit " or " exit
leaves. Line history is kept in
.I ~/.acv_history
between sessions.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
# Display
acv show measured.csv reference.acv
acv show -w 180 measured.csv

# Transform with explicit output
acv gamma 2.2 input.acv output.acv
acv offset 0 input.csv output.acv

# Transform with default output (TTY -> % display; non-TTY -> stdout CSV)
acv gamma 2.2 input.csv

# Preserve x-grid from the first input
acv compose -p sparse.csv correction.acv out.csv

# Contrast about a pivot other than 128
acv contrast -v 200 1.5 input.csv output.csv

# Reverse the input axis, as if the image were inverted before printing
acv invert correction.acv correction-inverted.acv

# Look at where a measured response loses separation
acv gradient 8 measured.csv

# Pipeline
cat measured.csv | acv smooth 12 - - > smoothed.csv
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR chart (1)
